      "title": "The Right Model. The Right Place. The Right Cost. | Embracing Digital Transformation",
      "summary": "The Right Model. The Right Place. The Right Cost. | Embracing Digital Transformation Embracing Digital Home Guidance Briefings (EDTW) What changed Interviews (EDT) How others did it Architectural Deep Dives What to do next Learn Digital Transformation Overview Pillars AI, Cyber, Data, Edge, Computing, Comms ODXA Domains &amp; mappings Whitepapers Monthly canonical synthesis Articles Explainers &amp; analysis Lectures (DTA) Learning series Intelligence Community Community Newsletter Patreon LinkedIn About About Host Sponsors Shop Search Skip to content Home Episodes Episode 28: The Right Model. The Right Place. The Right Cost. Episode 28 The Right Model. The Right Place. The Right Cost. 2026-10-14 Hosted by Dr. Darren Pulsipher Explore more in the episode archive . Summary In this episode of Digital Transformation Architect, Dr. Darren discusses how organizations can make thoughtful AI architecture decisions by focusing on real workloads and measurable outcomes rather than just model selection. He explains how to match AI models to deployment locations—device, edge, data center, cloud, or hybrid—based on factors like data sensitivity, latency, cost, and control. The episode also covers common AI architecture patterns, the importance of embedding trust and controls in the design, measuring cost per useful outcome, and stress-testing the architecture before deployment. Listeners are encouraged to use the AI Workload Decision Canvas to map workloads, surface assumptions, and prioritize actions toward scalable, trustworthy AI solutions. AI architecture AI deployment Artificial Intelligence Generative AI AI trust AI governance AI workload Enterprise AI Hybrid AI Retrieval-augmented generation Agentic workflows Cost optimization Digital transformation In this episode of Digital Transformation Architect, Dr. Darren discusses how organizations can make thoughtful AI architecture decisions by focusing on real workloads and measurable outcomes rather than just model selection.",
